#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d7c30c2-5d42-5648-ae49-5a0c7ec43233" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "e4893301-34d3-5f63-9d1c-715588d98d40" :question/math-academy-id "e-9531" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." :question/worked-solution "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad y = \\sin{t}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj = \\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&=-\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= - (1 - \\cos^{2} t) + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 2\\cos^{2} t - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F(r) \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2t]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 4π - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179528 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "a448227d-9bf3-54dd-8639-cbdc56a5a403" :question/math-academy-id "e-9532" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = y^3 + 5\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (t^{3} + 5)i + tj, 0 \\le t \\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad y = t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj \\\\\n&= ti + (t^{3} + 5)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} + 5)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 3t^{2}i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (ti + (t^{3} + 5)j) \\cdot (3t^{2}i + j) \\\\\n&= t \\cdot 3t^{2} + (t^{3} + 5) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} + t^{3} + 5 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4t^{3} + 5dt \\\\\n&= [t^{4} + 5t]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5 - 0 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/id #uuid "9152629f-47a8-5a71-9bd9-3f5205788f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9533" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 2j + t[i - 2j] \\\\\n&= ti + (2 - 2t)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = 2-2t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= (1 - y)^{2}i + xj \\\\\n&= (1 - (2 - 2t))^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (2t - 1)^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (4t^{2} - 4t + 1)i + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2 - 2t)j \\\\\n&= i - 2j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= ((4t^{2} - 4t + 1)i + tj) \\cdot (i - 2j) \\\\\n&= (4t^{2} - 4t + 1) \\cdot (1) + (t) \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 4t^{2} - 6t + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4t^{2} - 6t + 1dt \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{3}t^{3} - 3t^{2} + t) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{3} - 3 + 1) - 0 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95e28c9e-2228-50d4-b676-f6484c1b9e88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{3 - 2t} + \\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177647} {:answer/id #uuid "10691e6c-af5a-594a-b543-d69d9c8da3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{2 + 3t} + \\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177648} {:answer/id #uuid "2050ccfb-94cc-5c91-b0d9-4c7a9da71943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}e^{-2t} + \\ln (3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177649} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc7f10b-b993-57a8-9231-98c8d45d434a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}3e^{2 + 3t} - 2\\ln (3 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177650} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff8c510-3cf3-51a4-8eff-d968f9f33374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1} - 2e^{3 - 2t} + 3\\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff8c510-3cf3-51a4-8eff-d968f9f33374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1} - 2e^{3 - 2t} + 3\\ln (2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177651} :answer-field/id #uuid "a261db06-f27f-544f-817f-62483c77b794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177646}] :question/id #uuid "88b31475-15e6-5485-a3fc-dfd5113d0f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-124701" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = e^{x}i + \\ln yj$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(3, 2)$ to the point $B(1, 5)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = (3 - 2t)i + (2 + 3t)j$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0983e1e6-46a9-53de-83f5-a28d5b1eee47" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Line Segments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ea158e-ca2a-5e5b-8ecd-b2fa2a21a9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177653} {:answer/id #uuid "e8439413-3db2-5587-a745-3d8cdeed25d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177654} {:answer/id #uuid "eae912eb-e824-5f79-b270-5672633c8fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177655} {:answer/id #uuid "0befd065-e9be-574b-abba-b2bce297a583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177656} {:answer/id #uuid "53a8508e-7bbf-53fe-9d00-f271624998ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eae912eb-e824-5f79-b270-5672633c8fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177655} :answer-field/id #uuid "578f5370-21a2-5f59-b58d-cbb06fd9058c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177652}] :question/id #uuid "f0f71dad-242f-524e-855f-4cf34a64efd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124702" :question/problem "Evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + π\\cos πyj$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6eddefb-d4b9-5e5b-9278-94de0d8dbff6" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868724c5-e34e-5f63-97f9-e5b667d01d2e" :answer/type 